    "problem_statement": "Err.map() returns None instead of self, breaking the Result type contract\n\nDescription\n\nAfter a recent change, the `map()` method on `Err` objects no longer works correctly. When calling `map()` on an `Err` object, it returns `None` instead of returning itself, which breaks the contract of the Result type.\n\nSTR:\n1) Create an `Err` object\n2) Call `map()` on it with any function\n3) Try to access the error value\n\nExpected:\n```python\nerr = Err(\"error message\")\nresult = err.map(str.upper)\nassert result.err() == \"error message\"  # This should work\n```\n\nActual:\n```python\nerr = Err(\"error message\")\nresult = err.map(str.upper)\n# result is None instead of an Err object\n# Trying to call .err() on it raises:\n# AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'err'\n```\n\nThis breaks the contract of the Result type, where `map()` should always return a Result object (either Ok or Err). The issue affects any code that expects to be able to chain operations on Result objects, as the chain will break if an Err is encountered."
  }
